# patches/09-amd64-spike2-exceptions.patch — SPIKE 2, BOTH HALVES: a 64-bit IDT
# and a working context switch. Applies on top of 08-amd64-spike1-trampoline.patch.
#
# Verified by: ./smoke-openbios.sh amd64-fault
#              ./smoke-openbios.sh amd64-ctx
#
# EXCEPTIONS. arch/amd64 had no IDT: the first fault was a triple fault and the
# machine vanished -- exactly how Spike 1's SSE bug presented. A 64-bit gate is
# SIXTEEN bytes with the handler offset split over three fields, so
# init_exceptions is new code rather than a port of x86's.
#
#   0 > 0 100000000 !
#   Unexpected Exception: page fault @ 08:...
#   Faulting address: 0000000100000000
#
# A null trap was built and GIVEN BACK: page 0 on a PC holds the real-mode IVT
# and the BIOS Data Area, and this firmware's console reads the BDA to find the
# VGA CRTC port, so unmapping it faults during boot. Faulting above 4 GiB
# demonstrates more anyway -- a 32-bit firmware cannot form the address.
#
# CONTEXT SWITCH. THE FRAME IS THE STACK: push everything, hand the stack
# pointer to __context, take what was there, pop. The pointer swap IS the
# switch. Two things the 32-bit original did are simply gone, and both follow
# from Spike 1's finding that long mode ignores segment bases:
#   * no sgdt/lgdt in the frame -- the firmware and any client share the one
#     flat GDT the trampoline built, and relocate() is a no-op;
#   * no lretq and no segment pushes -- CS/SS never change, and `push %ds` does
#     not encode in long mode.
# No xmm saves either: SysV makes xmm0-15 CALLER-saved and this is reached by a
# `call`, so Spike 1's SSE enable costs the switch nothing. That was worth
# checking, because the opposite was the obvious guess.
#
#   0 > test-ctx-switch . switching to new context:
#   5a  ok
#
# KNOWN LIMIT, still: after a FAULT the machine survives and prints " ok" but
# the outer interpreter accepts no further input. The context switch working
# does not fix it -- they are different mechanisms -- and amd64-fault asserts
# the limit is still the limit so that fixing it cannot pass unnoticed.

