+	/* ---- Spike 2 tried a null-pointer trap here, and REVERTED it -------
+	 * The first 2 MiB was re-done as 4 KiB pages with page 0 left absent,
+	 * so that `0 0 !` would fault. It faults, all right -- during boot,
+	 * before the prompt exists:
+	 *
+	 *   Unexpected Exception: page fault @ 08:00000000001034c3
+	 *   Faulting address: 0000000000000000
+	 *   rdx: 3f8   rsi: 3d5
+	 *
+	 * Those two registers name the culprit: 0x3d5 is the VGA CRTC data
+	 * port, and the console code reads its address out of the BIOS DATA
+	 * AREA at 0x400-0x463 -- which is INSIDE PAGE 0. On a PC, page zero is
+	 * not spare: it holds the real-mode interrupt vector table and the BDA,
+	 * and this firmware legitimately reads the latter.
+	 *
+	 * So a null trap costs the BDA, and buying it would mean teaching the
+	 * video code to stop reading it. That is a real change with a real
+	 * benefit and it is not Spike 2's question. The identity map stays
+	 * whole; the exception checkpoint faults ABOVE 4 GiB instead -- an
+	 * address a 32-bit firmware could not even name, which demonstrates
+	 * rather more than a null write would have.
